<h2>India's largest guar gum market — and its most bifurcated buyer base</h2>
<p>The United States takes more Indian guar gum than any other country, roughly USD 96 million in 2024. What makes this market distinctive isn't just the volume — it's that US demand genuinely splits between two different industries buying two different specifications. Food and pharmaceutical manufacturers want a refined, food-grade thickener and stabilizer. Oilfield service companies want a higher-viscosity industrial grade for hydraulic fracturing fluid. Knowing which buyer you are changes the spec conversation from the first message.</p>

<h2>Why drilling activity matters to some buyers and not others</h2>
<p>Guar gum is a key thickening agent in fracturing fluid, so the industrial-grade segment of US demand has some real correlation with US drilling activity — it isn't a fixed, steady demand curve the way food-grade thickener demand tends to be. If you're sourcing industrial-grade guar gum for oilfield services, that cyclicality is worth building into your planning; if you're a food or pharma buyer, it's largely irrelevant to you.</p>

<h2>Documentation differs by grade, not just by country</h2>
<p>US-bound guar gum doesn't carry the EU's SHEFEXIL-endorsed PCP testing requirement — that's an EU-specific documentation step tied to a 2012 contamination episode, not something the US requires. Food/pharma-grade shipments do go through standard FDA prior-notice procedure like any US food import; industrial-grade shipments not entering the food supply generally carry lighter documentation requirements.</p>

<h2>Logistics</h2>
<p>Transit runs 25–35 days to East Coast ports — long, but guar gum's 18–24 month shelf life in sealed, dry storage absorbs that easily. Guar is also one of the few products we supply with genuinely no seasonal supply gap, since it's processed and stored year-round from the autumn kharif harvest.</p>

